batch-app/markers/*
/.idea/
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CardDemo test-suite generated outputs (finding MI-06).
# WHY: these paths are PRODUCED by the runner scripts (scripts/build_test_*.sh,
# scripts/run_*.sh) and must never be committed. The policy is intentionally
# NARROW -- it names only generated artifacts, so it cannot mask a real source
# file. The mutable per-run test WORKSPACE and its loaded VSAM INDEX files are
# already created OUTSIDE the repo (under $TMPDIR by scripts/test_env.sh, per
# finding MA-04), so no workspace/index rule is needed here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compiled COBOL programs / shared modules (CARDDEMO_BUILD_DIR default).
/build/
# JUnit XML + coverage reports (CARDDEMO_REPORTS_DIR default).
/reports/
# gcov line-coverage artifacts from the GnuCOBOL-transpiled C (--coverage build).
# Kept global (not just under /build/) so a manual gcov run in a source tree is
# also covered; the GnuCOBOL --save-temps intermediates themselves are written
# into CARDDEMO_BUILD_DIR (/build/, already ignored above), so no separate .c
# rule is needed here.
*.gcno
*.gcda
*.gcov
# Python-harness coverage data (coverage.py; parallel mode writes .coverage.*).
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
# pytest / Python bytecode caches.
.pytest_cache/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Migration-target build output, local toolchain state and secrets-bearing files.
# WHY: the modernization work introduces four new build trees (services/, ui/,
# data-migration/, infra/) whose toolchains all write generated output INTO the
# working tree. Adding these rules up front means a build run can never stage
# generated bytes, and -- the reason this block is not merely tidiness --
# Terraform state and local .env files can never be committed at all. Both
# routinely contain plaintext credentials: `terraform.tfstate` stores every
# resource attribute including generated database passwords, and `.env` is by
# convention where local secrets live. The project's hard constraint is "NO
# secrets committed to repository", so the ignore rule is the structural
# enforcement of that constraint rather than a reliance on reviewer vigilance.
#
# Alternatives considered: per-tree .gitignore files (services/.gitignore,
# ui/.gitignore, ...). Rejected because those trees do not exist yet, so the
# protection would only start applying once someone remembered to author them --
# exactly the window in which an accidental `git add -A` after a first build is
# most likely. A single root policy is protective from the first commit onward.
#
# Trade-off: the patterns are broad (unanchored `target/`, `node_modules/`,
# `dist/`) rather than anchored to a specific tree, so they also ignore
# same-named directories anywhere in the repo. That is accepted deliberately:
# every one of these names is a build-output convention, none is used for source
# anywhere in this repository, and breadth is what makes the rule survive the
# monorepo layout the migration introduces.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Java / Maven build output (services/**, one target/ per module plus the root).
target/
# Node build output and installed packages (ui/**).
node_modules/
dist/
# Python virtual environments. Python's own venv module writes a self-excluding
# .gitignore inside the directory it creates, but only for that exact directory
# and only on Python >= 3.11; naming the conventional locations here means an
# environment made by any other tool, or under any of these names, is also safe.
.venv/
venv/
env/
# Terraform local state, plugin cache and plans. `*.tfstate*` covers the state
# file, its backup and any workspace-suffixed variant; `*.tfplan` covers saved
# plans, which embed the same resource attributes as state.
.terraform/
.terraform.lock.hcl.bak
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan
crash.log
# Local environment files. `.env.example` is explicitly re-included because it
# is a COMMITTED template that documents variable NAMES with no values.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
